WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:16.080
[Singing, praying in native language and drumming.]

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:25.109
[Jewel James:] We're up here in the Mount
Baker Snoqualmie United States Forest and...

00:00:25.109 --> 00:00:29.419
...behind me is an old growth western red
cedar.

00:00:29.419 --> 00:00:33.960
At one time the cedars went all the way to
the shoreline and ...

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.780
...were a couple thousand years old.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:44.370
The trees were so thick, you couldn't walk
through the forest, the old growth.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:51.239
Most of the forest has been logged, and so
there's quite a few old growth cedars yet,

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:54.210
but they're hard to come by...

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:59.680
...and so we worked with the US Forest Service
here, and there's one tree that fell from

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:00.680
this spot.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:08.940
It got undermined by all the wind and the
rain and the snow, and it finally fell.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.520
But this tree behind me is also going to fall,
and it's undermined.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.830
About sixty percent of its root wad is exposed
to the elements, and so when we talked to

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:19.830
the Forest Service...

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:24.700
...they said we could have this log for the
National Library of Medicine project, as well.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:31.740
And so we're really fortunate to have some
elders from the Lumbee nation and some trained

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.390
traditional young persons...

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.471
...from the Navajo nation that have come here
to help us pray for this.

00:01:37.471 --> 00:01:48.780
This tree here.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.820
The one that's already down.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:53.770
[Singing and drumming.]

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:59.100
[Elder:] We come here today to pay honor.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:04.500
This man that's going to take you home, not
to take your life...

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:13.030
...but to give you new life, to transform
you, that you'll bring home peace to our people

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.050
throughout the nation.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:20.560
[Singing and drumming.]

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:30.190
[Jewel James:] When we come and take a tree
out of the forest, we're supposed to be appreciative.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:36.660
We're supposed to be respectful, and we're
supposed to humbly ask permission to take

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:37.720
this tree...

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:44.311
...because the forest is full of medicine,
and the cedar tree is just one more of the

00:02:44.311 --> 00:02:53.680
medicine people.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.790
This totem pole is for the National Library
of Medicine, and the story is about protection

00:02:58.790 --> 00:02:59.870
and preservation...

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:03.280
...of traditional knowledge and traditional
medicine.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.541
Totem poles are about symbols and the symbols...

00:03:06.541 --> 00:03:13.110
...usually tied us -- stories, legends, myth,
folklore it ties to some of the traditional

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.110
knowledge within...

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:15.280
...a native community.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:21.209
They all have their stories about their relationship
with the earth.

00:03:21.209 --> 00:03:31.100
And in that relationship over time, they learned
about medicine.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.240
We're pretty excited about participating with
the National Library of Medicine.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:40.400
We know that they have a national library
as well as regional, branches of the National

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:41.970
Library and...

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:48.380
...they also make themselves available and
participate with local universities on different

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.290
projects and programs.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:57.530
And so as a native community, we know that
we have culture, and we know that we have

00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:59.260
traditional knowledge.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:04.840
We know that we have knowledge handed down
from generation to generation about traditional

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:05.840
medicine...

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.040
...but we don't know how to protect it.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:13.000
Most of the time, we've learned over the centuries
to keep it quiet.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:19.039
And so we're hoping that the journey as we
go to the various native communities will

00:04:19.039 --> 00:04:20.620
create awareness...

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:25.940
...you know, and that we'll come to understand
that the National Library of Medicine is a

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:27.879
resource...

00:04:27.879 --> 00:04:35.830
...that it has information that we can access,
utilize, also, that it's not a one-way relationship.

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:37.300
It's not them coming to usů

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.960
...and asking to document and catalogue everything
we know.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.090
It's them coming to us and saying this is
what we know.

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:45.120
You know, you're welcome to it.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:05:10.009
I think it's a good public institution that
has a basic open door and is transparent.

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:22.819
[Flute playing.]

00:05:22.819 --> 00:05:32.860
[Narrator:] For native peoples, healing is
more than the treatment of physical symptoms.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:40.490
You see, native healing is intertwined with
larger concepts, such as self-respect, spiritual

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:41.620
balance...

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:46.169
...family, and a connection with the land.

00:05:46.169 --> 00:05:52.779
That's why for native peoples individual healing
cannot be separated from...

00:05:52.779 --> 00:05:56.009
...community wellness; they're one and the
same.

00:05:56.009 --> 00:06:00.630
In this exhibition we explore these native
concepts of...

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.900
...health and wellness.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:09.250
Honoring the native tradition of oral history
we'll gather the chorus of healing voices...

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:17.599
... from across the country so that you may
hear their stories in their own words.

00:06:17.599 --> 00:06:24.729
[Flute playing.]

00:06:24.729 --> 00:06:35.349
[Narrator:] A reverence for nature is common
among all native peoples.

00:06:35.349 --> 00:06:37.819
Unlike modern society which erects barriers...

00:06:37.819 --> 00:06:42.590
...between itself and the natural world, native
cultures derive strength and healing from

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:44.550
the land and water.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:50.979
Individual wellness cannot be achieved when
the connection to nature is missing or contaminated.

00:06:50.979 --> 00:06:57.939
You'll hear personal stories about the importance
of land, water, the environment, and the medicinal

00:06:57.939 --> 00:06:58.939
plants...

00:06:58.939 --> 00:07:02.970
...that have been a source of healing for
millennia.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.589
So this is [in Hawaiian language].

00:07:05.589 --> 00:07:13.449
This is [in Hawaiian], and this is the [in
Hawaiian] of the green tea leaf.

00:07:13.449 --> 00:07:15.520
This is for a broken bone.

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:21.249
[Narrator:] Native peoples believe that each
person has a responsibility for his or her

00:07:21.249 --> 00:07:23.969
proper behavior and health.

00:07:23.969 --> 00:07:32.159
These stories offer ideas about personal responsibility,
family and tribal ties, role models, and identity.

00:07:32.159 --> 00:07:36.210
[Katherine Gottlieb:] Our native men are still
leaders in our communities.

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:40.960
In days of old, they would stand and protect
their families.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:47.050
They would die for their women and their children
and what we're saying is do that again.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:54.080
Come back and do it today, and this time do
it around domestic violence, child sexual

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:55.719
abuse, and child neglect.

00:07:55.719 --> 00:08:01.470
End that for us as strong men and warriors
just like they did in the days when they had

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:03.310
to stand up and fight for us.

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:05.789
We want them to do it today.

00:08:05.789 --> 00:08:12.360
[Narrator:] The gathering of the community
is a cherished practice because in a traditional

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.129
society the community...

00:08:14.129 --> 00:08:17.210
...is a source of support and healing.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:21.139
For native peoples' individual health cannot
be separated from...

00:08:21.139 --> 00:08:22.910
...community wellness.

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:27.539
You'll hear about the differences between
life on the reservation and life in the city...

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:31.620
...and attitudes that native people share
regardless of where they're living.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:37.659
[Ralph Forquera:] The displacement of native
people to cities has had a profound effect

00:08:37.659 --> 00:08:41.000
on just their psyche, how they see the world...

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:46.920
...how they view the world, their ability
to trust people, their ability to interact

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:51.959
in a way that allows us to be able to...

00:08:51.959 --> 00:08:58.920
...instill in them a sense of belonging, a
sense of wellbeing that can translate into

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:01.270
better health.

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:07.250
[Narrator:] Native peoples live in two worlds,
one that honors the way of their ancestors

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:08.899
and one that acknowledges...

00:09:08.899 --> 00:09:13.649
...the conveniences and challenges of modern
society.

00:09:13.649 --> 00:09:15.250
Wellness is sought through the preservation...

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:21.009
...of languages, ceremonies, and burial practices,
reverence for the creator, and respect for

00:09:21.009 --> 00:09:22.009
the wisdom...

00:09:22.009 --> 00:09:26.339
...of elders are important traditions that
have survived for millennia.

00:09:26.339 --> 00:09:29.230
You'll also hear stories of loyalty and...

00:09:29.230 --> 00:09:34.120
...military service that exemplify modern
native traditions of service and patriotism.

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:39.580
[Thomas Begay:] It was 0900 when we hit the
beach.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:45.690
We maintained communication under all kinds
of fire, snipers...

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:47.280
...you name it.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:50.690
The officer or somebody writes the message.

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:54.470
All we do -- it's an endless stream of messages...

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:58.300
...and all we do is transmit into Navajo.

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:01.350
In Navajo there would be another talker.

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:09.550
We sent close to 800 in four to eight hours
-- came out perfect.

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:14.769
[Narrator:] Taken together these concepts
influence a native person's approach to good

00:10:14.769 --> 00:10:16.779
health and inform their decisionů

00:10:16.779 --> 00:10:22.560
ů whether to pursue Western medicine or traditional
healing practices when confronted with an

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:23.560
illness.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:30.290
[Senator Inouye:] Well, I'm glad we're beginning
to recognize officially the worth of these

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:31.940
native doctors.

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:39.269
And I'm glad we're doing this in Native American
-- the Indians, also.

00:10:39.269 --> 00:10:48.509
The Congress of the United States is considering
native sovereignty measures, which would give

00:10:48.509 --> 00:10:49.620
them...

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:53.100
...federal government recognition as we give
to Indians.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:57.459
Once that is set up, I think one of the high
priorities...

00:10:57.459 --> 00:11:07.029
...would be to set up a native hospital to
revive the practice of native medicine, for

00:11:07.029 --> 00:11:08.029
example.

00:11:08.029 --> 00:11:09.550
I think that's a good thing.

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:11.140
[Flute music playing.]

